<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kojiki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/712">712</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kojiki ('Record of Ancient Matters') is the oldest surviving Japanese chronicle, compiled by Ō no Yasumaro and presented to the court in 712. It recounts the myths of the creation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the descent of the imperial line from the sun goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amaterasu">Amaterasu</a>, followed by legends and genealogies of the early emperors. It is a founding text of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kojiki.jpg" alt="Kojiki" width="320" height="539" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ken'yu (賢瑜), Koten Hozonkai (古典保存会) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shinpukuji-bon_Kojiki_(%E7%9C%9F%E7%A6%8F%E5%AF%BA%E6%9C%AC%E5%8F%A4%E4%BA%8B%E8%A8%98).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to its preface, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-tenmu">Emperor Tenmu</a>, who reigned from 673 to 686, ordered a review of the clan records and commissioned a court attendant of exceptional memory, Hieda no Are, to memorise the records and oral traditions of the imperial line. On the 18th day of the 9th month of 711 Empress Genmei ordered Ō no Yasumaro to write down what Hieda no Are had learned, and he presented the work on the 28th day of the 1st month of 712. Earlier compilations of 620 had been lost when Soga no Emishi's estate burned in 645.</p><p>The Kojiki collates different traditions into one official mythology. It justifies the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamato-court">Yamato court</a> and gives other groups a place in the national genealogy, as when it absorbs the local traditions of the Izumo lineage in order to subordinate them to Yamato. It does not mention <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and scholars tend to treat it as myth rather than history.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The book is written mostly in Classical Chinese, but its songs and poems are in Old Japanese, written phonetically with Chinese characters. The first of its three volumes covers the creation and the age of the gods. Izanagi and Izanami form the islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and bear many deities, and Izanami dies giving birth to the fire god Kagutsuchi. Amaterasu, Tsukuyomi and Susanoo are born as Izanagi washes his left eye, his right eye and his nose. Ninigi, a grandson of Amaterasu, descends to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a> and becomes the ancestor of the imperial line.</p><p>The second volume begins with the conquests of Jimmu, the first emperor, and ends with the 15th emperor, Ōjin. The third volume covers the 16th to 33rd emperors and has far fewer dealings with gods.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian period</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki">Nihon Shoki</a> was widely studied, while the Kojiki was treated as a secondary text. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period few people had access to it, and its earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, the Shinpukuji-bon, dates from 1371 to 1372. It reached a wide audience only in print, with the Kan'ei Kojiki published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1644.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> scholars of nativist studies such as Kada no Azumamaro and Kamo no Mabuchi saw it as a record of a Japanese identity that could be recovered, and it gained the standing of a sacred text. Its myths, with those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki">Nihon Shoki</a>, inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> practices such as the misogi purification ritual.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kojiki” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Code of 701 created a Council of Kami Affairs, the Jingikan, beside the Council of State; and the court commissioned the <b>Kojiki</b> in 712 and the Nihon Shoki in 720, which set down the myths of the kami and the descent of the emperors from the sun goddess.</blockquote><a href="/religion/shinto">Shinto · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>These stories come from the <b>Kojiki</b> and the Nihon Shoki, the country’s first written records, set down between the 6th and 8th centuries CE after Japan had adopted Chinese characters, and they show little or no relation to what archaeology has found of Jōmon culture.</blockquote><a href="/culture/jomon-culture">Jōmon culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>In 710 the court moved to the planned capital of Heijo-kyo at Nara, and the first histories, the <b>Kojiki</b> of 712 and the Nihon shoki of 720, presented the emperors as descendants of the sun goddess.</blockquote><a href="/state/ritsuryo-japan">Ritsuryo Japan</a></li></ul></section>
